WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.680
Astronomers have fresh clues about the irregular dimming of tabby star that once hinted at alien structures. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 219. The star KIC 8462852 shows brightness drops of up to 20% at irregular intervals. It is an F-type star in sickness with a red dwarf companion. Citizen scientists from the Planet Hunters Project first flagged the signal in data from the Kepler telescope. Current theories include dust belts, comet swarms,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.420
or debris from a destroyed planet. New observations aim to distinguish internal stellar processes

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.500
from external material. The irregular pattern rules out regular planetary transits.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.220
Follow-up observations could distinguish between dust and comet scenarios. The star's binary

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.360
nature adds another layer to the analysis. Each new measurement narrows the range of possible

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.280
causes. The involvement of citizen scientists highlights the role of public participation

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:07.140
in astronomy. On a different note, NASA has asked private contractors to build a nuclear reactor capable of surviving space travel and operating without maintenance near the Moon's South Pole.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.240
The target launch date is December 2030. Russia aims to have a reactor operating on the Moon by 2036 as part of a partnership with China.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.560
The reactor must function during the 14 Earth Day long lunar night when solar panels cannot generate power.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.420
temperature. Temperatures during the night plunge to minus 130 degrees. The goal is to beat Russia

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:35.420
and China in the race for lunar nuclear power. Gleece 12b orbits its star every 12.8 days and is

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:41.640
similar in size to Venus. Its approximate surface temperature is 42 degrees, much lower than most

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:48.100
exoplanets. The host star is approximately 27% the size of the sun, with a surface temperature close

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:54.520
to 60% that of our star. The planet receives 1.6 times more energy from its star than Earth does

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.440
from the Sun. Observations were made using NASA's test satellite. The star does not show extreme

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:07.020
activity, which is important for preserving any atmosphere. LHS 1140B is a super-Earth with an

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:13.680
estimated mass about 5.6 times that of Earth, and a radius approximately 1.73 times larger.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.820
It orbits its red dwarf host star every 24.7 days at a distance of 49 light years.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:25.220
The planet is losing helium from its upper atmosphere at a rate of between 200 and 400

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.120
tons per second.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.180
No trace of hydrogen was detected in the atmosphere.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.780
The discovery was made using transmission spectroscopy.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.580
This provides an opportunity to study how exoplanet atmosphere is evolve over time.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.180
A planet the size of Earth passing in front of a star 50 light years away blocks only 100

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.840
of 1% of the starlight, yet that sliver carries the chemical signature of an entire atmosphere.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.680
During transit, the starlight filters through the planet's upper layers

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:57.320
where different molecules absorb specific wavelengths and leave dark lines in the spectrum.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.480
Instruments spread that light across ultraviolet to infrared bands and measure the depth

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.820
of each absorption feature to identify helium, hydrogen, or even complex organics.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.260
The method works because the planet's atmosphere acts like a temporary filter

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.260
that changes with altitude and temperature.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.360
One surprising number is that helium escape rates of several hundred tons per second

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.560
have already been recorded on worlds 49 light years away.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.820
We can see the gas leaving, but nobody knows whether the flow will strip the entire atmosphere

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.500
or leave a denser layer behind.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.100
Honda is teaming up with space infrastructure firm RedWire to develop an automated laboratory

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.160
support system.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.600
Astronaut time on the International Space Station is the most limited resource.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.940
future commercial stations will be smaller with crews of only two or four. RedWire is providing the

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.520
reaching and motion platform known as Stark. RedWire already manages eight operational science

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:58.420
facilities on the ISS. Automation will be essential for keeping experiments running during uncrewed

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:05.020
periods. The Artemis II crew launched on April 1st, 2026 and spent nine days one hour and 32

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:10.180
minutes in space. Through photographs, spoken observations, drawings, and annotations,

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:16.200
the four astronauts built a scientific record of the lunar far side. Images captured active weather

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:22.760
systems, auroras at both poles, atmospheric layers, a seharon dust plume, zodiacal light,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.620
and city lights on Earth. The data is now released as the Artemis-2 preliminary lunar science report.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.860
The mission provided the first crude opportunity for such observations since the Apollo era.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.980
Astronomers have potentially detected the first known second-generation planet around White

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:42.500
Dwarf HS0209 plus 0832.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.940
The Jupiter-like planet likely formed from accretion of debris cast off by the dying star.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:54.080
The White Dwarf atmosphere contains zinc, copper, aluminum, silicon, titanium, and niobium

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.840
at levels more than 1,000 times out of the sun.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.060
The planet orbits on an extremely short period of 4.4 days.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:06.080
Archival Hubble and Fuse data plus test observations supported the finding.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.060
This marks the first time a planet has been shown to form after a star's main sequence

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:11.480
phase.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.980
Looking back over the past week, NASA's Lunar and Crude Exploration efforts have stayed

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.700
front and center from fast commercial crew rotations to fresh, far-side imagery.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:27.520
Fascinating frontiers has tracked both long-standing mysteries like Tabby Star and brand new

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.540
find such as ancient radio galaxies and young dwarf systems. Space X and ESA milestones,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.060
including record transit times and crew handovers, continue to shape the cadence of operations

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.860
aboard the station. Next week we'll bring further details on the helium escape measurements

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:50.240
from LHS 1140B, as additional telescope time is scheduled. That covers today's Space

